  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now Kostya and Nastya are tied up alongside the others in the room. Kostya begs to be untied, though Chef Barinov reminds him how bad he is at keeping secrets, and then the inspector asks to use the restroom.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Шеф, как-то это совсем ненадёжно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это чтобы она не сбежала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, пожалуйста, отпустите нас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы точно никому не расскажем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя в прошлый раз просил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, это был смешной случай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Так что правильно нас связали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Мне сейчас в туалет нужно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ага, смотри какой он хитрый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Так делают, когда хотят сбежать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодец, Сеня, хорошая мысль.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Иди с ним и проследи.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Шеф, как-то это совсем ненадёжно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, this is somehow completely insecure.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Chef Barinov' and finishing on 'unreliably'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это чтобы она не сбежала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is so she does not run away.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'this' and 'so that' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, пожалуйста, отпустите нас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, please let us go.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'please' before landing on 'us'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы точно никому не расскажем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will definitely not tell anyone.' Kostya anchors the line on 'we', while 'to tell' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя в прошлый раз просил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I asked you last time.' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'asked'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, это был смешной случай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, that was a funny incident.' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'case' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Так что правильно нас связали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So they tied us up rightly.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'so' before landing on 'tied up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Мне сейчас в туалет нужно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I need to go to the restroom right now.' In Russian, the idea of 'to me' flows naturally into 'needed' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ага, смотри какой он хитрый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Aha, look how sly he is.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yeah' and finishing on 'clever'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Так делают, когда хотят сбежать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'People do that when they want to escape.' Notice how Russian expresses 'so' and 'to escape' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодец, Сеня, хорошая мысль.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well done, Senya, good thought.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'well done' before landing on 'thought'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Иди с ним и проследи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go with him and keep watch.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'go' and 'with' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
